const note=(id,domain,letters,title,essentials,sections,steps,example,traps,question,answer,refs=[])=>({id,domain,objectives:letters.split('').map(c=>`${domain}.${c}`),title,essentials,sections,steps,example,traps,question,answer,advanced:depth[id],sources:[...refs,...(id==='privacy-by-design'?['privacy']:[])],version:STUDY_VERSION});
export const lessons=[
note('capture-at-source',1,'cdf','Capture at the point of work',[
 'Choose the capture point from the business event and evidence needed, before choosing a scanner.',
 'Separate day-forward capture, backfile conversion and capture on demand.',
 'Verify completeness, legibility, metadata and successful handoff; an image alone is not a managed record.'
],[['Understand the concept','Capture brings information under deliberate control. The source may be paper, an email, a web form, a mobile photo or a transaction. Identify who creates it, when it becomes evidence, what context must accompany it and which system receives it. Earlier capture can reduce loss, but collecting everything indiscriminately increases noise and privacy exposure.'],['Choose a conversion approach','Day-forward handles new incoming material. Backfile conversion tackles an existing collection and needs selection, preparation and reconciliation. On-demand conversion responds to actual requests and avoids processing unused files, but creates variable turnaround. A mixed approach often fits better than one rule for every document.'],['Quality and exceptions','Specify acceptance criteria by information type: missing pages, unreadable text, wrong orientation, duplicate items and mismatched identifiers need different controls. Sample according to risk; critical fields may require full validation. Keep an exception queue with an owner rather than silently passing low-quality items downstream.']],
 ['Identify the event, required evidence and authoritative destination.','Compare volume, retrieval demand, urgency, cost and sensitivity.','Reconcile batches, validate metadata and route failed items for correction.'],
 'A clinic has ten years of rarely requested files and hundreds of new forms daily. Capture new forms at intake, convert historical files when requested, and prioritize any frequently accessed historical subset. Scanning every old page first would delay the current operational need.',
 ['Scanner speed does not prove end-to-end throughput.','Do not destroy originals merely because scanning finished; apply approved requirements and quality controls.'],
 'A scanned batch has the expected file count. Is it complete?','Not necessarily. Reconcile documents and pages, identifiers, content quality and destination acknowledgements. One file may contain missing or duplicated pages.'),
note('document-control',1,'eg','Document control and choosing a repository',[
 'Version history, check-out, approval and access control solve different problems.',
 'Choose capabilities against business requirements rather than a product label.',
 'File synchronization is not evidence that retention, audit and disposition controls exist.'
],[['Control a shared document','Versioning records changes; check-out can prevent conflicting edits; approval marks a decision; permissions control who may act. A team may need simultaneous editing rather than exclusive locking. Define what makes a version authoritative and how users distinguish drafts from approved publications.'],['Compare solutions','A specialized tool can fit one function closely but create integration and oversight gaps. A broader content platform can share controls across functions but require greater implementation effort. File-sharing tools simplify distribution; assess their actual governance, export, audit and lifecycle features instead of assuming they have none or all.'],['Evaluate the whole lifecycle','Test capture, retrieval, collaboration, approval, preservation and exit. Include accessibility, mobile use, offline behavior and administrator effort. Ask whether required controls survive downloading, external sharing and migration. A polished demonstration with sample files cannot establish that production requirements are met.']],
 ['Write the control and user requirements.','Demonstrate realistic scenarios and exceptions.','Evaluate integration, operational ownership and exit costs alongside features.'],
 'Engineering needs approved drawings traceable to a release. A shared folder with overwrite permissions makes the latest upload easy to find but does not establish approval. Use controlled versions, release status and an audit trail, with appropriate permissions.',
 ['The newest timestamp does not necessarily identify the approved version.','Buying a larger platform does not automatically fix inconsistent working practices.'],
 'What does check-out primarily prevent?','Concurrent conflicting edits to the controlled item. It does not by itself provide approval, retention or the right access model.'),
note('collaboration',1,'hijkl','Collaboration across teams and organizations',[
 'Keep shared context, ownership, permissions and authoritative versions clear.',
 'Match synchronous and asynchronous work to time zones, urgency and accessibility.',
 'Govern decisions and supporting artifacts, not just final attachments.'
],[['Work without losing context','Synchronous work supports immediate discussion but depends on availability. Asynchronous work supports distributed schedules but needs clear requests, deadlines and decision records. A meeting recording can contain evidence, yet a searchable summary with responsible owners may be more useful operationally. Determine what must be retained rather than recording everything by default.'],['Share deliberately','External sharing requires knowing the audience, data sensitivity, authentication method, permitted actions and access end date. A link is easier to revoke than an uncontrolled attachment, but downloaded copies may remain. Confirm how guest accounts, subfolders and inherited permissions behave.'],['Govern collaboration spaces','Email attachments produce competing copies and fragmented approvals. A shared workspace can improve coordination if its owner, classification, membership review and closure process are defined. Closing a project must preserve important decisions while removing access that is no longer needed. Governance should fit how people actually work.']],
 ['Identify participants, decisions and evidence.','Set access, version and approval rules.','Review membership and transfer required evidence before workspace closure.'],
 'A supplier project ends next month. Before its guest workspace closes, assign an owner to export required decisions and attachments, verify context and permissions, and arrange ongoing retrieval. Merely keeping the final presentation loses the reasons for key decisions.',
 ['A collaboration tool is not automatically the system of record.','A public link is not appropriate simply because the project deadline is close.'],
 'Why is asynchronous collaboration useful for a global team?','People contribute across time zones, provided decisions, deadlines and versions are explicit. It does not remove the need for access control and accountability.'),
note('preservation',1,'abmnop','Preserve usable information over time',[
 'Bit integrity, readability, authenticity and context are separate preservation concerns.',
 'Migration changes formats or platforms; emulation recreates an earlier operating environment.',
 'An archival format is one control in a preservation program, not a complete strategy.'
],[['Understand failure modes','Storage can degrade, hardware can become unavailable and software can stop understanding a format. A checksum helps detect changed bits but cannot show that the original content was accurate or still renders meaningfully. Preserve context, dependencies, ownership and evidence of transformations alongside the object.'],['Choose a response','Prefer well-documented formats suited to the content and required behavior. A fixed-page format may preserve a report but not a complex application. Migration makes content usable in a new environment and needs validation of meaning, structure and functionality. Emulation can retain interactions but introduces dependencies on the emulation environment.'],['Operate preservation','Inventory formats and dependencies, assess risk, keep appropriate redundant copies, check integrity and test retrieval. Record provenance and planned transformations. Test restoration and rendering before retiring an old system. Define who monitors technology changes and funds future work. Backups protect recovery; preservation also addresses continuing interpretability.']],
 ['Identify required content, context and behavior.','Assess format, media and dependency risks.','Validate transformations and periodically test usable access.'],
 'A contract archive is backed up every night, but its viewer license will expire. More backups do not solve the readability risk. Evaluate supported export or migration, verify content and signatures as applicable, and retain provenance before the viewer disappears.',
 ['PDF/A does not guarantee authenticity or replace retention decisions.','A matching checksum does not prove that a document can be opened.'],
 'When might emulation be preferable to format conversion?','When original behavior or interactions are essential and conversion would lose them, subject to managing and testing the emulation dependencies.',['preservation']),
note('ownership-and-selection',1,'qrs','Authority, drafts and migration selection',[
 'Define which system owns the authoritative evidence and which copies serve other purposes.',
 'Drafts may have evidential value; their status depends on context, not the filename.',
 'Select migration scope using value, obligations, risk and verified ownership.'
],[['Resolve competing copies','The system of record is the designated authoritative source for a category or transaction. Ownership is accountability for decisions about the information. Both must be clear when departments maintain different copies. An operational copy can be useful without being authoritative. Document how changes propagate and how discrepancies are resolved.'],['Handle working material','A draft containing unique approvals, negotiations or substantive comments may need retention. A convenience copy with no additional value may not. Apply approved categories and holds rather than a blanket rule to delete drafts or keep all versions forever. Maintain enough history to explain the final decision.'],['Decide what moves','Profile the collection, identify redundant or obsolete material, resolve unknown ownership and check holds before selection. Compare full transfer, phased transfer, selective transfer and continued read-only legacy access. Each has cost and risk. Record excluded categories and defensible reasons; verify that users can still retrieve required evidence.']],
 ['Assign authority and accountable ownership.','Classify finals, substantive drafts and convenience copies.','Approve migration scope and reconcile selected, moved and excluded items.'],
 'Two departments each claim their spreadsheet is the master customer list. Migrating both without resolving authority preserves the conflict. Establish ownership, reconcile differences and define the authoritative dataset before designing the transfer.',
 ['Moving everything can transfer unnecessary risk and defects.','A file called FINAL does not establish authority.'],
 'May all old drafts be excluded from migration?','Only after assessing their evidential value, retention requirements and holds. Age or draft status alone is insufficient.'),
note('metadata',2,'abcde','Metadata that supports decisions',[
 'Metadata should enable an actual task: finding, understanding, securing or managing information.',
 'A field needs a definition, allowed values, ownership and a maintenance rule.',
 'Automation reduces entry effort but still needs validation and correction.'
],[['Build a workable model','Descriptive metadata supports identification and retrieval. Administrative metadata supports management, such as ownership or restrictions. Technical metadata describes format and system properties. The categories overlap; the important issue is whether the values support the required decisions consistently. A file creation date may differ from the business event that starts retention.'],['Choose how values enter','Users can enter context, systems can inherit values, workflows can supply event dates, and recognition tools can propose extracted fields. Inheritance saves effort but can spread a wrong classification. Keep provenance and correction routes for important values. Make fields mandatory only where needed and reliably obtainable.'],['Maintain across systems','Mappings must preserve meaning, datatype, controlled terms, identifiers and cardinality. A multi-valued field cannot safely become a single free-text string without an explicit rule. Validate completeness, accuracy and consistency, monitor exceptions and assign stewardship for changes. A schema alone does not guarantee good data.']],
 ['Start from retrieval and control decisions.','Define field meanings, sources, validation and owners.','Test mappings and maintain quality after capture.'],
 'A retention rule begins when a contract ends. Importing the file upload date into an expiry field creates premature or late disposition. Keep upload date separate, obtain the actual contract end event and handle missing events explicitly.',
 ['More mandatory fields can reduce quality if users invent values.','Identical field names can hide different meanings.'],
 'What is wrong with inheriting a department code forever?','An organizational move can make it stale. Define whether the field represents origin or current ownership and update according to that meaning.'),
note('taxonomy',2,'fghr','Classification, taxonomy and findability',[
 'A taxonomy organizes concepts; metadata applies useful descriptions to items.',
 'A stable concept identifier is different from the label users see.',
 'Search, navigation and classification work together; none automatically replaces the others.'
],[['Choose a structure','A hierarchy arranges broader and narrower concepts. A thesaurus can also connect preferred labels, synonyms and related concepts. Functional classification groups work by what the organization does; an organization chart groups by current departments and may change frequently. Choose according to the task and the stability required.'],['Design with stakeholders','Users supply language and retrieval needs, records staff supply lifecycle requirements, security and privacy teams identify restrictions, and technical teams implement mappings. Pilot with realistic tasks. A technically elegant structure that users cannot recognize will encourage workarounds and misclassification.'],['Connect to governance','Classification can support navigation and trigger policies, but a subject label should not silently imply every retention or security rule. Define policy relationships explicitly. Maintain ownership, change approval, synonyms, deprecated terms and mappings between versions. Test how changes affect existing items and downstream systems.']],
 ['Collect terms from real work and retrieval tasks.','Define concepts, labels, relationships and owners.','Pilot findability and test policy mappings before broad rollout.'],
 'Users search for both HR and People Operations. One stable concept can have a preferred label and an alternative label so both terms find the same material. Creating duplicate categories would fragment the collection.',
 ['A folder path alone may not capture multiple relevant concepts.','An identical label does not prove two concepts are equivalent.'],
 'Why can functional classification outlast a department hierarchy?','Business functions can remain stable when departments are renamed or reorganized. The design still needs review when the underlying work changes.',['vocabulary']),
note('extraction',2,'ijk','Recognition, extraction and validation',[
 'OCR recognizes characters; extraction identifies and structures useful values.',
 'A confidence score is not proof of business correctness.',
 'Validate high-impact fields against business rules and authoritative sources.'
],[['Distinguish the stages','A scanned page is an image. Recognition proposes text; classification identifies a document type; extraction identifies fields; validation checks values; integration sends accepted data into a process. Each stage can fail independently. A barcode can reliably identify a form yet say nothing about the accuracy of its handwritten amount.'],['Choose the right source','For structured applications, use supported exports, APIs or database interfaces with approved access where practical. Re-reading screenshots may discard reliable structure. For images, assess scan quality, layout variability, language and handwriting. Preserve relationships between the extracted value, source page and business transaction.'],['Handle uncertainty','Combine format checks, range checks, cross-field comparisons and reference-data lookups. Route ambiguous values to trained reviewers. Measure both extraction accuracy and the cost of false acceptance. Calibrate thresholds with representative documents; a single threshold for names and payment amounts may be inappropriate.']],
 ['Identify the source and target data requirements.','Select recognition or structured extraction methods.','Validate, reconcile and route exceptions before business action.'],
 'An invoice engine reads 8,500 as 850 with high confidence. The purchase-order comparison catches the mismatch. Better confidence reporting alone would not have prevented a wrong payment; business validation matters.',
 ['OCR text does not automatically make a document accessible or correctly classified.','Automation can reproduce systematic errors at high volume.'],
 'What should happen to a low-confidence bank-account field?','Do not accept it blindly. Use risk-appropriate verification and an authorized correction process before payment action.'),
note('analytics',2,'lm','Analytics and machine learning in context',[
 'Choose the technique from the business question and acceptable error.',
 'Separate training data, validation data and genuinely independent evaluation.',
 'Monitor deployment behavior; a good test score is not permanent assurance.'
],[['Match capabilities to work','Classification assigns categories, entity extraction identifies items such as organizations, and clustering groups similar material without predefined labels. Descriptive analysis explains what happened; predictive methods estimate outcomes under assumptions. A prediction does not establish causation. Choose interpretable rules when they solve the problem adequately.'],['Evaluate errors','Precision asks how many positive predictions are correct. Recall asks how many actual positives were found. Increasing one can reduce the other. Missing a restricted document and unnecessarily restricting an ordinary document have different consequences. Evaluate by class and real operating prevalence rather than relying only on overall accuracy.'],['Keep people accountable','Training labels can be inconsistent, historical patterns can be biased and new content can differ from training content. Assign a model owner, document intended uses and limits, validate with realistic cases and monitor drift. Use human review for consequential or ambiguous decisions and maintain a correction process.']],
 ['Define the decision, affected people and error costs.','Evaluate representative data and class-specific measures.','Deploy with monitoring, review thresholds and ownership.'],
 'Only 1% of a collection is sensitive. A model calling everything ordinary scores 99% accuracy but misses every sensitive item. Recall for the sensitive class is zero, making the model unsuitable for that protection task.',
 ['High average accuracy can hide failure on a rare important class.','More data does not fix unsuitable labels or an ill-defined goal.'],
 'If missed sensitive documents are the main risk, which measure deserves close attention?','Recall for sensitive content, alongside precision, review capacity and the consequences of false positives.',['ai']),
note('search',2,'n','Search that retrieves the right evidence',[
 'Application search covers its own context; enterprise search spans multiple governed sources.',
 'Security trimming must honor the requesting user’s current access.',
 'Relevance, freshness and completeness are different quality measures.'
],[['Understand the search chain','Connectors obtain content, parsers extract text, indexes organize it, queries retrieve candidates and ranking orders results. Failure can occur at any stage. A document missing from results might not be indexed, might use unexpected terms, might be restricted or might rank too low. Diagnose before replacing the search engine.'],['Extend across sources','Enterprise search can reduce repeated searches but must map identifiers, access rules, metadata and update behavior across repositories. Indexing creates another governed copy of information. Content removals and permission changes must reach the index. A result snippet can disclose information even when opening the document is blocked.'],['Improve with evidence','Use realistic search tasks, known relevant documents and user feedback. Controlled terms, synonyms and useful facets can improve retrieval. Compare precision and recall against the task. In legal or audit collection, broad coverage may matter more than a short attractive results list.']],
 ['Check source coverage, indexing and permissions.','Test terms, metadata, filters and ranking.','Measure task success and remediate the specific failure.'],
 'A former team member cannot open a restricted report but can still read its indexed preview. Fix authorization at retrieval and snippet generation and synchronize permission changes; hiding the open button is insufficient.',
 ['An empty result does not prove no responsive information exists.','Adding semantic search does not remove access-control requirements.'],
 'What must change when a source document is deleted or restricted?','The search index and any cached previews must reflect the approved change within the designed update process.'),
note('migration',2,'opq','Migrate content without losing meaning',[
 'Reconcile content, context, permissions, relationships and holds, not just file counts.',
 'A migration needs acceptance criteria, exceptions, cutover and rollback.',
 'Third-party sources require workable export and continuing access arrangements.'
],[['Prepare before moving','Inventory repositories, owners, formats, dependencies, duplicates and restrictions. Profile quality before mapping fields. Define scope and exclusions with accountable approval. Identify identifiers and relationships that other applications depend on. Unknown ownership and unresolved holds are decisions to resolve, not technical details to ignore.'],['Validate in layers','Test a representative pilot, including unusual formats, large items, multiple versions and restricted content. Reconcile counts and checksums where suitable, then verify metadata meaning, permissions, rendering, links and business retrieval. A successful import log establishes transfer activity, not usable equivalence.'],['Control the transition','Choose a freeze, delta synchronization or phased cutover that fits business continuity. Define who corrects exceptions, when rollback is triggered and when legacy access may end. For partner services, personal devices or social platforms, address contractual access, export formats, privacy, deleted material and provenance before the access window closes.']],
 ['Inventory and approve scope and mappings.','Pilot, reconcile and obtain user acceptance.','Cut over with rollback and verified legacy retirement criteria.'],
 'All contracts transfer, but their links to amendments break. File counts match while business meaning is lost. Include relationship checks in acceptance, repair mappings and delay legacy retirement until required retrieval succeeds.',
 ['Do not retire the source just because the upload reached 100%.','A vendor retention promise is not a usable export or retrieval plan.'],
 'Which is stronger evidence of success: matching file counts or tested business retrieval?','Both contribute, but neither is sufficient alone. Combine completeness and integrity reconciliation with context, permissions and real retrieval tests.'),
note('llm',2,'s','Use language models with evidence and controls',[
 'Fluent text can be unsupported; verify claims against reliable evidence.',
 'Retrieval can improve grounding but does not guarantee correctness or permission safety.',
 'Treat prompts, retrieved documents and outputs as governed information.'
],[['Understand the capability','A language model generates plausible continuations from patterns. It can assist with drafting, summarization and question answering, but can invent facts or references. It is not automatically an authoritative database. Use a defined task, appropriate context and an evaluation set that reflects actual operating conditions.'],['Ground answers carefully','Retrieval-augmented generation supplies selected material as context. The retrieval layer must enforce access; the answer must be checked for support and correct attribution. Stale or irrelevant passages can lead to wrong answers. Untrusted source text can attempt to redirect the system, so separate evidence from instructions and limit downstream actions.'],['Govern the use','Know whether the service retains inputs, trains on them or transfers them to other locations. Minimize sensitive material, evaluate outputs and define human approval for consequential actions. Track model and configuration changes, monitor failures and make it easy to report unsupported responses.']],
 ['Define an allowed use and data boundary.','Test support, privacy and unsafe instruction handling.','Require appropriate review and monitor changes.'],
 'An assistant drafts a policy answer with a convincing citation, but the linked document says the opposite. Require passage-level support and human verification before treating the answer as policy; polished wording is not evidence.',
 ['A citation can exist and still fail to support the claim.','A private chat interface does not establish the provider’s retention terms.'],
 'Does adding a document search tool eliminate hallucinations?','No. Retrieval quality, source authority, permission checks and verification of the generated claims still matter.',['ai']),
note('process-discovery',3,'abde','Choose the right process to improve',[
 'Define the problem and desired outcome before automating.',
 'Understand variation, rules, exceptions and ownership.',
 'Prioritize value and feasibility together, including nonfinancial benefits.'
],[['Find the actual problem','A process connects inputs, activities, decisions and outputs toward an outcome. Complaints about slow software may actually reflect missing information, unclear authority or repeated rework. Establish boundaries and identify the customer. Separate the organization’s internal convenience from the user’s successful outcome.'],['Assess suitability','Stable, frequent and rule-based work can be a good automation candidate. Variable judgment and unclear rules may need redesign or case support first. Consider volume, error risk, integration, exception rate and data quality. Do not assume every process must be fully automated to improve.'],['Establish the baseline','Measure current turnaround, waiting, effort, errors and outcomes before claiming benefits. Interview participants and observe actual work. Evaluate compliance, consistency and service quality as well as cost. A low-volume high-risk process can deserve priority even when labor savings are modest.']],
 ['Define outcome, boundary and owner.','Measure current work and exception causes.','Compare redesign options and select a manageable pilot.'],
 'Invoices wait four days because nobody owns exceptions. A faster routing engine would move the same unresolved items into a new queue. Define decision authority and escalation rules before automating.',
 ['Automating a defective process can make defects spread faster.','High volume alone does not establish a worthwhile business case.'],
 'What should precede choosing workflow software?','A clear business problem, process understanding, baseline measures and requirements.'),
note('process-mapping',3,'fghi','Map and diagnose the real process',[
 'Combine interviews, observation and data; a diagram is a model, not complete reality.',
 'Show handoffs, decisions, exceptions and information dependencies.',
 'Distinguish touch time from waiting and total elapsed time.'
],[['Gather evidence','Interviews reveal intent and constraints; observation reveals workarounds; logs reveal timing and volume; customer journey analysis shows the experience across organizational boundaries. Each has blind spots. Include frontline staff, downstream recipients and exception handlers instead of asking only the manager.'],['Build a useful map','Set start and end boundaries, identify actors, show activities and decisions, and label the conditions on branches. Swimlanes can clarify responsibility and handoffs. Distinguish the current state from the proposed future state. Document assumptions where work differs by location or case type.'],['Troubleshoot systematically','Look for queues, repeated entry, missing inputs, rework and unnecessary approvals. A map often omits variation, capacity and informal behavior; add measurements. If one step is slow, ask whether it is the constraint or simply waiting for an earlier dependency. Optimizing a nonconstraint may not improve overall flow.']],
 ['Triangulate interviews, observation and event data.','Map current normal and exception paths.','Measure delays and test the proposed cause before redesign.'],
 'A map shows a ten-minute review, but cases spend three days in its queue. Cutting review effort to eight minutes may barely change lead time. Investigate queue ownership, capacity, batching and missing inputs.',
 ['A neat future-state map does not prove implementation feasibility.','A department view can hide delays experienced by the customer.'],
 'Why use process logs as well as interviews?','They can reveal actual frequencies, waits and exceptions that participants may forget or describe differently. Logs also need context and quality checks.',['process']),
note('workflow-design',3,'cjkl','Routing, workflow and measurable performance',[
 'Sequential work has dependencies; parallel work needs a defined join rule.',
 'Deadlines require escalation and exception handling, not just timestamps.',
 'Measure outcomes and quality alongside speed.'
],[['Choose the scope','Routing sends information or tasks to a destination. Workflow coordinates activities and decisions. Business process management extends to modeling, measurement, ownership and continuing improvement. Product labels overlap, so test the required behavior rather than relying on the label.'],['Design the path','Independent reviews may run in parallel; dependent decisions must wait for prerequisites. Specify whether all reviewers, any reviewer or a defined quorum is required. Handle rejection, reassignment, absence, duplicate messages and timeout. Keep evidence of who acted, on what version, with what authority.'],['Read metrics correctly','Cycle or lead time measures elapsed time under a defined boundary; touch time measures active effort. Throughput counts completed units per interval. First-pass yield measures completion without rework. A rising completion count can hide declining quality or a growing backlog. Define denominators and time boundaries consistently.']],
 ['Identify dependencies and decision authority.','Define routing, joins, deadlines and exception paths.','Monitor throughput, waiting, backlog, quality and business outcomes.'],
 'Legal and finance can review a contract independently, but execution requires both approvals of the same version. Parallelize review, then join only after both approvals. A changed contract must follow the defined reapproval rule.',
 ['Parallel processing does not mean the first approval always wins.','A timestamp without escalation does not ensure action.'],
 'Throughput improved but complaints rose. What else should be examined?','Errors, rework, unmet service needs and the nature of completed cases. More completions alone do not prove a better outcome.',['process']),
note('rpa-and-cases',3,'mnop','Choose RPA, workflow or case management',[
 'RPA executes repeatable interactions; case management supports evolving knowledge work.',
 'Stable rules and interfaces matter for RPA reliability.',
 'A case can combine human decisions, documents and automated steps.'
],[['Use automation where it fits','RPA often interacts with existing application screens, reproducing a sequence of user actions. It can help where supported integration is unavailable, but screen changes and unexpected dialogs make it fragile. Evaluate APIs or native integration when practical. Bots require access limits, monitoring and maintainable exception handling.'],['Support variable work','Case management organizes work around a case, its evidence, status and outcomes. The next action may depend on new information or professional judgment rather than a predetermined sequence. Cases still need controls, deadlines and accountability. Flexibility does not mean unrecorded or unlimited discretion.'],['Combine capabilities','A complaint case may use RPA to retrieve routine account data, workflow to obtain a required approval, and a case worker to investigate an unusual allegation. Select the capability per activity. Consider auditability, service accounts, credential management and continuity when automation fails.']],
 ['Identify rule stability, variation and judgment.','Prefer supported integration where it meets requirements.','Combine automated tasks and controlled human decisions.'],
 'A benefit appeal can require different evidence for each applicant. Case management supports the changing investigation. A bot may collect standard account details, but should not force every appeal through one rigid path or make unsupported decisions.',
 ['RPA is not a substitute for redesigning unclear rules.','Case management can contain structured workflows.'],
 'Which task is the stronger RPA candidate: recurring transfer between stable screens or a novel investigation?','The recurring rule-based transfer, assuming controls and exceptions are manageable. The investigation needs flexible evidence and judgment.'),
note('signatures',3,'qr','Electronic signatures and digital assurance',[
 'Electronic signature is the broader concept; digital signatures use cryptographic mechanisms.',
 'Identity, intent, integrity and the surrounding evidence all matter.',
 'Legal acceptability depends on the transaction and applicable requirements.'
],[['Separate the concepts','A typed name, a click-to-sign action and a scanned handwritten mark can be electronic signing methods. A cryptographic digital signature binds a signing operation to specific data using keys and verification mechanisms. A visible signature image alone does not establish that the file is unchanged or identify the signer reliably.'],['Assess assurance','Evaluate how the signer is authenticated, how intent is recorded and which exact version is signed. Protect private keys and assess certificate trust, validation information and timestamps where relevant. Cryptography can support integrity and attribution, but operational misuse or weak identity proofing can undermine the overall process.'],['Plan long-term evidence','Keep the signed object and required audit evidence, not only a screenshot. Determine how signatures can be validated after software, certificates or algorithms change. Different jurisdictions and document types can impose different requirements; do not apply a universal legal-validity rule to every signing method.']],
 ['Identify transaction requirements and risk.','Choose authentication and integrity controls.','Retain verifiable evidence and test future validation.'],
 'A PDF shows a pasted signature image, but anyone could replace the contract text underneath it. A digital signing process with appropriate identity and validation controls offers stronger evidence of document integrity than the image by itself.',
 ['Digital signatures do not necessarily encrypt the document’s contents.','A technically valid signature alone does not establish every legal condition.'],
 'What property is checked when signature verification detects changed content?','Integrity of the signed data. The full trust decision also involves identity, key protection and the signing context.',['security']),
note('governance',4,'abcdef','Governance, stewardship and accountability',[
 'Governance assigns decision rights; stewardship carries out accountable management.',
 'Inventory information, systems and processes for different but connected purposes.',
 'Senior management needs decisions, risks and measurable outcomes, not only policy counts.'
],[['Start with context','Information governance coordinates value, risk, responsibility and lifecycle decisions. Determine the organization’s business, jurisdictions, contractual obligations and risk tolerance. Avoid copying another organization’s schedule or framework without checking whether its assumptions fit. Policies need implementable controls and named owners.'],['Assign roles','An executive sponsor provides authority and resources. Information owners make accountable business decisions. Stewards maintain quality and definitions. Legal, records, privacy, security and IT contribute distinct expertise. Champions encourage adoption; a center of excellence can provide shared methods, while a community of practice shares experience. Clarify escalation when responsibilities overlap.'],['Assess and review','An information inventory describes assets and their context; a system inventory describes applications and technology; a process inventory describes work. Link them to reveal unmanaged flows. Assess current policies, actual behavior, control evidence and capability gaps. Review progress with senior management so competing priorities and residual risks receive decisions.']],
 ['Establish business context and obligations.','Assign decisions, execution and escalation.','Inventory assets and evaluate controls using evidence.'],
 'IT can identify where a dataset resides but cannot alone determine its business retention need. The owner works with records and legal specialists; IT implements the approved control and provides evidence of operation.',
 ['A policy owner and a system administrator are not automatically the same role.','A complete policy library does not prove compliance in practice.'],
 'Why connect information and system inventories?','To identify where each asset is processed, who owns it and which controls apply, including copies and third-party flows.'),
note('security-controls',4,'gy','Match security controls to the threat',[
 'Least privilege and need to know restrict access according to the task.',
 'Encryption, redaction and rights controls address different risks.',
 'Verify effective protection across copies, exports and administrative paths.'
],[['Apply access deliberately','Role-based access groups permissions by job function. Attribute-based rules can consider factors such as classification, location and purpose. Minimize permissions, review them after role changes and separate incompatible duties. Authentication establishes a claimed identity; authorization determines permitted actions. Neither removes the need for monitoring and accountability.'],['Choose complementary controls','Encryption protects data under defined conditions but depends on key management; an authorized user can still disclose plaintext. Redaction removes selected content from a released version and must remove hidden text and metadata as appropriate. Rights controls can restrict certain actions in supported environments, but cannot guarantee that every external copy remains controlled.'],['Verify operation','Log important access and administrative changes, review suspicious activity and test revocation. Treat service accounts and exports as part of the access model. Secure backup copies and indexes as well as the main repository. Control selection should follow risk and business needs rather than whichever feature is easiest to enable.']],
 ['Identify asset, threat and permitted purpose.','Select layered access and content controls.','Test hidden data, exported copies and revocation.'],
 'A black rectangle hides a salary on screen, but copying the text reveals it. Use actual redaction, sanitize the derivative and verify both visible and hidden content before release.',
 ['Encryption does not make unnecessary collection appropriate.','Removing a user from one group may not remove access through another.'],
 'Does authentication prove a user may read every document?','No. Authorization must evaluate the requested action and applicable access rules.',['security']),
note('privacy-by-design',4,'hijkm','Privacy from collection through disposal',[
 'Collect and use personal information for defined, justified purposes.',
 'Minimization, restricted defaults and deletion planning belong in design.',
 'A privacy assessment evaluates people’s risks, not only cybersecurity.'
],[['Identify the information','Personal information can identify someone directly or through combinations. Sensitivity depends on context as well as category. An employee number may link to a person even without a name. Pseudonymized data can remain personal when reidentification is possible; aggregation also needs disclosure-risk assessment.'],['Design the lifecycle','Map collection, use, sharing, storage and disposal. Ask which fields are necessary, who needs access, how long purposes remain valid and which rights or notices apply. Privacy-friendly defaults reduce unnecessary exposure. Security protects information, while privacy also asks whether collecting and using it is appropriate.'],['Assess before launch','Describe the processing and affected people, identify risks, evaluate mitigations and record accountable decisions. Involve appropriate business, privacy, legal and security participants. Reassess when purpose, scale, vendors or technology change. Good inventories and metadata make requests and controlled disposal more practical. Specific legal duties depend on the applicable jurisdiction.']],
 ['Map data, purpose and recipients.','Remove unnecessary collection and define controls.','Assess residual risks and approve before significant change.'],
 'A training dashboard needs completion rates, not full birth dates. Do not collect those dates merely because an optional profile form supports them. Use the minimum necessary information and protect small-group statistics against reidentification.',
 ['Consent is not a universal substitute for every privacy requirement.','Removing names alone does not guarantee anonymization.'],
 'Why assess privacy even when storage is encrypted?','Encryption does not address unnecessary collection, incompatible use, excessive retention or the full impact on individuals.',['security']),
note('sovereignty-and-incidents',4,'ln','Location, jurisdiction and incident response',[
 'Data residency concerns location; sovereignty concerns applicable authority and legal control.',
 'Map remote support, backups and subprocessors as well as primary storage.',
 'Contain incidents, preserve evidence and evaluate notification duties promptly.'
],[['Understand cross-border processing','A dataset stored locally may still be accessed or processed elsewhere. Contracts, corporate control and local laws can affect obligations. Assess provider locations, backup locations, support access and transfer arrangements. There is no universal rule that local storage alone resolves all cross-border concerns. Use qualified advice for actual legal decisions.'],['Prepare a response','Define detection, escalation, containment, investigation, communication and recovery roles. Preserve relevant logs and evidence while limiting further harm. Not every security incident is a notifiable personal-data breach, but uncertainty should trigger timely evaluation rather than silence. Record decisions and the evidence available at the time.'],['Recover and improve','Validate the scope, affected information and exposure before making unsupported statements. Coordinate legal, privacy, security and business teams. Notification recipients and deadlines vary; use the applicable rules rather than memorizing one global deadline. Correct the cause, verify recovery and incorporate lessons into controls and training.']],
 ['Map locations, access paths and applicable obligations.','Contain and preserve evidence with assigned ownership.','Assess notifications, recover and document lessons.'],
 'A vendor stores files in-country but gives overseas support staff unrestricted access. The residency statement does not describe the full processing arrangement. Investigate access and transfer implications before approving the service.',
 ['Do not erase logs while attempting to clean up an incident.','A contract label does not establish that no cross-border access occurs.'],
 'Is every lost laptop automatically a reportable breach everywhere?','No universal conclusion follows. Assess data, protections, exposure, jurisdiction and applicable notification requirements.',['security']),
note('records',4,'opq','Recognize records and automate responsibly',[
 'Record status comes from evidential purpose and obligations, not file format.',
 'Capture content together with enough context to explain the activity.',
 'Automated classification needs accountable rules, exceptions and verification.'
],[['Identify evidence','Records document activities, decisions, rights and obligations. They may be messages, data entries, videos or documents. A useful test is whether the information is needed to show what happened and under whose authority. Legal, historical, administrative and operational needs can all matter. The formal definition and obligations vary by organization and jurisdiction.'],['Keep trustworthy context','A decision without its date, participants, approved version or linked transaction may be hard to interpret. Capture the relevant metadata and relationships. Protect against unauthorized alteration and maintain evidence of authorized changes. Declaring an item as a record should fit the process rather than depend entirely on users remembering a final checkbox.'],['Automate with oversight','Rules can trigger capture, classification, retention and disposition consistently. Errors can also scale. Validate rule logic, monitor exceptions and assign owners for changes. Distinguish recordkeeping needs from backup recovery and avoid assuming a system becomes compliant simply because it has a records-management module.']],
 ['Identify what evidence the activity needs.','Capture context and define protection and ownership.','Validate automated rules and monitor their results.'],
 'A chat contains the only approval to release a product. It may be required evidence even though no formal approval document exists. Capture the decision and relevant context under the approved recordkeeping process.',
 ['An informal channel does not automatically make content a nonrecord.','A scanned copy is not automatically an approved replacement for every original.'],
 'Why is recordkeeping more than saving a file?','It includes context, authenticity, controlled access, lifecycle rules and retrievability as evidence.',['records']),
note('retention',4,'rstu','Retention triggers and defensible disposition',[
 'A retention rule combines category, trigger, period and final action.',
 'Legal, regulatory, contractual and justified business needs inform the rule.',
 'Check holds and approvals before disposition; there is no universal seven-year rule.'
],[['Build the schedule','Define the record category, scope, accountable owner, retention trigger, period and disposition instruction. Triggers may be closure, termination or another business event rather than file creation. Document the authority or rationale and review when obligations change. Conflicting requirements need accountable resolution rather than an arbitrary longest period for everything.'],['Calculate and control','A rule of six years after contract closure cannot run reliably when closure dates are absent. Link the system to the event or manage an exception queue. Keep holds separate from ordinary retention so eligible content is not destroyed while preservation duties apply. Require appropriate authorization and maintain disposition evidence.'],['Select the action','Disposition can mean authorized destruction or transfer to an archive. Choose methods suited to sensitivity, media and environment; verify effectiveness. Deleting a file reference may not remove recoverable content or independent copies. Address backups through documented cycles and restrictions rather than promising instant universal erasure.']],
 ['Classify and identify the authoritative trigger.','Calculate eligibility and check holds or exceptions.','Authorize, execute and document the appropriate action.'],
 'A case file is retained for a stated period after closure. Moving it to a new server does not restart the business retention clock. Preserve the closure metadata and distinguish migration dates from lifecycle events.',
 ['Keeping everything forever increases cost and exposure.','Retention expiry does not override a valid hold.'],
 'A record is past its scheduled date but on hold. What happens?','Suspend disposition for the held material and maintain control until an authorized release and renewed eligibility review.',['records']),
note('holds-and-discovery',4,'vwx','Legal holds and collecting reliable evidence',[
 'A hold suspends routine disposition for relevant information.',
 'Identify custodians, systems, scope and ongoing obligations.',
 'Preserve provenance and integrity through collection, review and production.'
],[['Put the hold into operation','An authorized hold needs a defined scope, responsible people, notices, acknowledgements and enforcement where applicable. Consider ongoing creation and changes in custodian or system. Monitor compliance and record releases. A hold does not require stopping every deletion in the organization regardless of relevance.'],['Collect across sources','Relevant evidence may be in shared drives, cloud services, mobile devices or partner systems. Address authority to collect, privacy, access limitations, export quality and expiring accounts. Preserve metadata and relationships; screenshots alone may omit searchable text or context. Work with appropriate legal and technical specialists.'],['Maintain a defensible trail','Document what was collected, where it came from, when it was obtained, who handled it and transformations performed. Use integrity checks as appropriate and preserve originals under controlled conditions. Review scope, privilege and relevance before authorized production. A chain of custody supports explanation of handling; it does not by itself prove every statement is true.']],
 ['Scope and implement the preservation obligation.','Collect authorized evidence with context and integrity controls.','Review and produce through a documented process.'],
 'A departing employee’s account will be deleted tomorrow while relevant messages are on hold. Coordinate preservation before deactivation or deletion; a general reminder email does not ensure the evidence survives.',
 ['Do not confuse preservation with unrestricted disclosure.','A hash detects changes relative to a reference, not the truth of the original content.'],
 'What distinguishes a hold from a retention schedule?','The schedule governs ordinary lifecycle timing; a hold overrides routine disposition for relevant material until authorized release.',['records']),
note('manage-in-place',4,'z','Govern content where it resides',[
 'Manage in place applies controls without moving all content into one repository.',
 'Control coverage and evidence matter more than physical centralization.',
 'Unsupported connectors and independent copies create governance gaps.'
],[['Understand the model','A governance layer can apply classifications, access policies, holds or disposition instructions to content in source systems. This can preserve established workflows and avoid migration cost. It depends on the source exposing sufficient control and reporting capabilities. A central policy screen does not prove every source actually enforced the rule.'],['Evaluate coverage','Test version scope, metadata synchronization, holds, access changes, disposition and audit evidence. Understand what happens during outages, connector failures and vendor changes. Some systems may support discovery but not reliable retention or deletion. Define exceptions and ownership for content outside supported coverage.'],['Compare architectures','Central repositories can simplify consistency but introduce migration and user-adoption burdens. Distributed controls can fit diverse applications but increase integration and coordination effort. A hybrid design may be appropriate. Choose according to required evidence, user needs and enforceable controls rather than a universal preference for centralization.']],
 ['Inventory sources and required controls.','Test actual enforcement and failure behavior.','Assign owners and monitor coverage gaps.'],
 'A policy console reports that a hold was sent to five systems, but one connector is offline. Treat that source as an exception requiring remediation and verified enforcement; the successful request log is insufficient.',
 ['Managing in place does not mean leaving information unmanaged.','Visibility into a source is not the same as authority to control it.'],
 'What is the key prerequisite for trustworthy manage-in-place governance?','Supported, testable enforcement in each source, with evidence, exception handling and accountable ownership.'),
note('strategy',5,'abcdh','Strategy, ownership and a realistic roadmap',[
 'Start with business outcomes and information problems, not a preferred tool.',
 'Distinguish success conditions, measures and the work needed to achieve them.',
 'A roadmap sequences capabilities and dependencies; it is not only a purchase calendar.'
],[['Define the destination','An information strategy connects business priorities to the information capabilities required. Explain who benefits, which risks or delays improve and how success will be judged. More engagement or more automation is not inherently better unless it improves a meaningful outcome. State assumptions and constraints explicitly.'],['Make delivery accountable','The sponsor resolves priorities and secures resources. Business owners define outcomes and acceptance, specialists design controls, IT delivers technical capability and users validate the working process. Plan effects on roles, approvals, training and support. A project affects ways of working as well as software.'],['Sequence change','A roadmap can begin with ownership and quality foundations, then pilot a capability, then expand. Identify dependencies, milestones, decision gates and funding. Include operational handover and ongoing improvement. Critical success factors are necessary conditions; indicators measure progress; targets describe the intended level or result.']],
 ['Connect a business outcome to required capabilities.','Assign sponsorship, ownership and success measures.','Sequence dependencies and decision gates in the roadmap.'],
 'Automated retention depends on reliable closure dates. Put event capture and ownership ahead of broad disposition automation; buying the lifecycle module first does not remove that dependency.',
 ['Counting installed licenses is not evidence of business value.','A roadmap needs revisions when evidence or dependencies change.'],
 'What makes a useful strategy measure?','It has a defined baseline, target, owner and interpretation tied to the intended business outcome.'),
note('baseline',5,'efg','Assess the organization and technology first',[
 'Assess culture, obligations and actual practices alongside architecture.',
 'Reuse an existing capability only if it meets the requirements and lifecycle needs.',
 'Separate verified facts from assumptions and unresolved gaps.'
],[['Assess the operating environment','Understand business priorities, regulatory context, ownership, skills and willingness to change. Observe real workarounds and information flows. A documented policy can differ from routine behavior. Identify whose work changes, what users value and which controls are difficult to operate. This establishes the starting point for a practical implementation.'],['Assess the estate','Inventory applications, repositories, interfaces, identities, formats and dependencies. Examine support status, capacity, availability and planned retirement. Evaluate data quality, access rules and export capabilities. A technically functional system near end of support may be a poor foundation for a long-term program.'],['Evaluate reuse honestly','Extending a current platform can reduce cost and adoption effort, but customization, integration and vendor dependency may offset savings. Compare fit, risk, total cost and future maintenance. Document gaps that require process change rather than software and validate key assumptions through evidence or a pilot.']],
 ['Gather business, people and control evidence.','Map applications, dependencies and lifecycle risks.','Compare reuse, extension and replacement against requirements.'],
 'A department proposes buying a second repository because search is poor. Assessment finds the current platform already supports the needed search, but metadata is inconsistent. Test metadata and configuration improvements before assuming replacement is necessary.',
 ['Existing does not mean suitable; new does not mean better.','A technical inventory alone misses adoption and accountability problems.'],
 'Why assess platform lifecycle stage?','Support, upgrade and retirement timelines affect sustainability, implementation risk and the value of additional investment.'),
note('business-case',5,'ijk','Business case, total cost and credible benefits',[
 'Compare alternatives against a measured baseline and stated assumptions.',
 'Include implementation and operating costs, not only license price.',
 'Separate cash savings, capacity released, avoided risk and qualitative value.'
],[['Build the comparison','Describe the problem, options including a credible current-state option, expected benefits, costs, risks and delivery approach. Use business owners to validate volumes and time estimates. A forecast should expose uncertainty rather than present every optimistic assumption as certain. State how benefits will be measured after implementation.'],['Count the costs','Include acquisition, integration, migration, cleanup, infrastructure, training, change support, ongoing administration, maintenance and eventual exit. Some are one-time and some recurring. A low subscription price can hide expensive implementation or support. Use comparable time horizons and avoid counting the same benefit twice.'],['Interpret value','Saving staff time may release capacity without reducing payroll. Reduced exposure is valuable but may be difficult to monetize reliably. Track service quality and compliance outcomes alongside financial measures. A simple ROI calculation is (benefit minus cost) divided by cost for a clearly defined period; timing and risk can require more sophisticated analysis.']],
 ['Establish the baseline and alternatives.','Estimate full lifecycle costs and attributable benefits.','Test assumptions and assign benefit owners.'],
 'A project costs 100,000 and is expected to yield 140,000 in comparable net operating benefits before that project cost. Simple ROI is 40%. If the benefit is only time made available, do not present it automatically as cash saved.',
 ['A benefit forecast is not a measured result.','Cost avoidance and direct savings should not be combined without explanation.'],
 'Why include the cost of doing nothing?','The current approach also has costs and risks. A fair comparison needs an explicit baseline rather than treating the status quo as free.'),
note('requirements',5,'lm','Requirements that can be tested',[
 'Business requirements explain the needed outcome; system requirements specify supporting behavior and constraints.',
 'Make requirements observable, prioritized and traceable to acceptance.',
 'Include exceptions, nonfunctional needs and accessibility.'
],[['Gather from several perspectives','Interview business users, observe tasks, examine records and include control and support specialists. Distinguish a stakeholder’s suggested solution from the underlying need. Resolve conflicting requirements with accountable owners. An unprioritized wish list creates uncertainty about what must be delivered.'],['Write for verification','A requirement such as fast search cannot be tested consistently. Define the collection, query, user load and acceptable response threshold. Functional requirements describe behavior; nonfunctional requirements cover qualities such as performance, availability, usability and security. Specify accessible interaction for users with different abilities and devices.'],['Maintain traceability','Connect the business need to design, tests and acceptance evidence. Include boundary conditions, invalid input, permissions and recovery from failure. Manage changes so their cost and impact are visible. Avoid vendor-specific wording unless there is a justified constraint; otherwise it can exclude suitable options unnecessarily.']],
 ['Elicit the need and prioritize with stakeholders.','Define measurable conditions and exceptions.','Trace each important requirement to an acceptance test.'],
 'Users ask for a new export button. Investigation reveals they need a monthly auditable report. A scheduled report may meet the need with less manual handling, but confirm its fields, permissions and evidence requirements before choosing.',
 ['A feature demonstration is not proof of every acceptance condition.','Nonfunctional requirements are not optional simply because they are less visible.'],
 'How can a vague requirement be improved?','Define who needs what behavior, under which conditions, to what measurable standard, and how acceptance will be demonstrated.',['accessibility']),
note('implementation',5,'nop','Implement, adopt and sustain the solution',[
 'Pilot representative work, including exceptions, before broad cutover.',
 'Change management includes participation, communication, training and support.',
 'Compare centralized and distributed designs against enforceable requirements.'
],[['Deliver in controlled stages','Translate approved requirements into design and configuration, integrate systems, prepare information and test. Use realistic volumes, permissions, failures and recovery. Obtain business acceptance with named criteria, prepare rollback and verify migration before retiring the source. Deployment is a technical event; successful operation needs ownership and monitoring.'],['Help people change','Explain why the change matters and how each role works differently. Use champions, role-specific practice and accessible support. Measure adoption through successful tasks and reduced workarounds, not only training attendance. Provide feedback routes and adjust the process when legitimate needs are missed.'],['Operate the architecture','A single repository can simplify common controls but requires consolidation and may not fit every application. Multiple repositories can preserve specialized workflows but require integration, common policy and consistent evidence. Define support, updates, backups, access reviews and vendor exit for the selected design. Handover should include accountable service ownership and benefit review.']],
 ['Pilot, test controls and obtain business acceptance.','Prepare communication, role-based training and support.','Cut over with rollback, then review service and benefits.'],
 'The application launches successfully, but users keep sending attachments because guest access is confusing. Treat the workaround as evidence for change support and usability improvement rather than declaring success from uptime alone.',
 ['Training attendance does not prove competent use.','A single repository is not automatically the lowest-risk architecture.'],
 'When is an implementation complete enough to hand over?','When agreed acceptance, operational readiness, ownership, recovery and support requirements are met, with residual issues explicitly accepted and tracked.')
];
export const lessonForObjective=objective=>lessons.find(l=>l.objectives.includes(objective));
export const libraryIndex=()=>lessons.map(({id,domain,objectives,title,essentials})=>({id,domain,objectives,title,summary:essentials[0]}));
